WEBVTT

NOTE SE201 Lesson 9: Agents That Operate Enterprise Systems — captions generated from the narration script

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.570
Software engineering 201. Lesson nine.

2
00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:06.720
Agents that operate enterprise systems.

3
00:00:07.070 --> 00:00:09.683
Picture a made-up billing system.

4
00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:12.851
Three in the morning, a refund goes out.

5
00:00:12.851 --> 00:00:15.384
The log says: admin. Who did it?

6
00:00:15.384 --> 00:00:18.552
The engineer on call? One of the agents?

7
00:00:18.552 --> 00:00:21.799
Or: the log can't say? The log can't say.

8
00:00:21.799 --> 00:00:24.570
People and agents share that login.

9
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.328
Lesson eight left three questions about a pipeline.

10
00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:30.267
Whose name does it run under?

11
00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:32.138
What is it allowed to touch?

12
00:00:32.138 --> 00:00:37.351
Who can see what it did? Add one from lesson five: where does a person decide?

13
00:00:37.351 --> 00:00:39.690
Together: who operates this system?

14
00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.383
In our made-up subscription-billing system, an agent works the nightly reconcile.

15
00:00:45.383 --> 00:00:48.549
Whose name? Its own identity: billing ops agent.

16
00:00:48.549 --> 00:00:50.857
Not a shared login, not a person's.

17
00:00:50.857 --> 00:01:00.092
So the log says who. Its job, written down: compare our ledger with the payment processor's report, work out why they differ, tell a person.

18
00:01:00.092 --> 00:01:02.730
And one named owner, who answers for it.

19
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.679
What may it touch? The least the job needs.

20
00:01:05.679 --> 00:01:08.579
It reads the ledger, and the processor's report.

21
00:01:08.579 --> 00:01:11.299
It writes in one place: a case, for a person.

22
00:01:11.299 --> 00:01:15.227
It can't change the ledger, move money, or change its own access.

23
00:01:15.227 --> 00:01:18.188
That's enforced by the system, not by the prompt.

24
00:01:18.188 --> 00:01:20.363
An instruction can be talked around.

25
00:01:20.363 --> 00:01:22.720
A permission that isn't there can't be.

26
00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:24.984
Some things are out of reach.

27
00:01:24.984 --> 00:01:29.032
The card number: the processor holds it, and we hold a token.

28
00:01:29.032 --> 00:01:35.270
The processor's key: a tool fetches the report, with a read-only key the agent is never shown.

29
00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.456
Customer details: only the fields the job needs.

30
00:01:38.456 --> 00:01:39.650
No name, no email.

31
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.117
One made-up night. Two forty A.M.

32
00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:45.773
The processor's report: nine hundred eighty-nine charges.

33
00:01:45.773 --> 00:01:48.210
Our ledger: nine hundred eighty-eight.

34
00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:54.047
The agent reads both, and finds the one: a thirty-dollar renewal, charged by the processor.

35
00:01:54.047 --> 00:01:58.730
Our side timed out, the retries ran out, and it never reached the ledger.

36
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:04.739
Add the missing entry? It can't change the ledger, and it doesn't look for a way around.

37
00:02:04.739 --> 00:02:06.733
Refund it, so the counts match?

38
00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:13.550
It can't move money. It opens a case, for the ledger's owner: what it found, the evidence, a proposed fix.

39
00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:14.450
Then it stops.

40
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.414
Who can see what it did? Every action left one line: when, who, what, why, result.

41
00:02:20.414 --> 00:02:26.096
The agent gives the why. The system writes the line, and the agent can't change it.

42
00:02:26.096 --> 00:02:31.162
Where does a person decide? At eight fifteen: the ledger's owner approves.

43
00:02:31.162 --> 00:02:34.379
The fix is a new entry, under the owner's name.

44
00:02:34.379 --> 00:02:36.570
Who did it? Now the log can say.

45
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:42.871
A near miss, also made up. At setup, this agent's access was copied from a support role.

46
00:02:42.871 --> 00:02:45.914
So it could issue refunds, and never said so.

47
00:02:45.914 --> 00:02:49.228
Then its owner reviewed every permission: needed?

48
00:02:49.228 --> 00:02:52.609
Used? Three stay. Refunds: not needed, never used.

49
00:02:52.609 --> 00:02:59.980
Removed. Agents read text they didn't write, like a note on a payment, and text can be written to steer them.

50
00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:03.294
With the spare permission, a refund could go out.

51
00:03:03.294 --> 00:03:05.390
Without it, the system says no.

52
00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:09.647
Two more, for a bad day. A limit: say, twenty cases a night.

53
00:03:09.647 --> 00:03:12.251
Past that, it stops, and tells a person.

54
00:03:12.251 --> 00:03:17.460
A stop switch: its owner turns off this one identity, and only this agent stops.

55
00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.231
So, to recap. One agent, one job, one identity.

56
00:03:21.231 --> 00:03:24.216
The least access, enforced by the system.

57
00:03:24.216 --> 00:03:28.074
Every action logged, where the agent can't change it.

58
00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:31.350
A person decides on money, and on the ledger.

59
00:03:31.700 --> 00:03:34.208
Which gives us rule nine, for your agents.

60
00:03:34.208 --> 00:03:37.909
Use only your own identity, and only the access the job needs.

61
00:03:37.909 --> 00:03:40.417
Never borrow a login, or work around a no.

62
00:03:40.417 --> 00:03:42.507
Give your reason with every action.

63
00:03:42.507 --> 00:03:46.805
Don't move money, or change the ledger, unless a person has released it.

64
00:03:46.805 --> 00:03:49.194
Otherwise: prepare it, and hand it over.

65
00:03:49.194 --> 00:03:51.940
If you can do more than the job needs, say so.

66
00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:56.198
Now, your turn. Pick one recurring job in a system you run.

67
00:03:56.198 --> 00:04:04.742
Ask your AI to draft its agent's job card: its identity, what it may do, what it can't, what it logs, and where a person decides.

68
00:04:04.742 --> 00:04:07.060
Then: what's the worst it could do?

69
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.975
An agent can operate the system.

70
00:04:09.975 --> 00:04:13.999
It can't answer for it. You still own the outcome.

71
00:04:13.999 --> 00:04:17.540
Next, the capstone: one feature, end to end.

72
00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.310
See you in lesson ten.
